{"t":1791536563,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"e5cbeb45-fb20-41b7-a0e3-2063b1a4458f","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-09T07:55:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 090755\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n355 AM EDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nLowered dew points for this afternoon a bit from NBM, otherwise\nno changes made. Main weather impact will come Sunday into\nSunday night as the remnants of Hurricane Isaias move through \nthe Mid- Atlantic.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Remnants of Hurricane Isaias to bring rain, breezy \n  conditions Sunday into Sunday night.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Remnants of Hurricane Isaias to bring rain, \nbreezy conditions Sunday into Sunday night.\n\nThe main weather impact to the region over the next week is going to \nbe from the remnants of what is now Hurricane Isaias. Model guidance \nis in generally good agreement that Hurricane Isaias' surface low \nweakens and broadens as the system moves into the TN Valley Saturday \nnight into Sunday. The mid-level circulation amplifies and becomes a \nstrong cutoff low over KY/TN that moves slowly east across the Mid-\nAtlantic Sunday into Monday. Periods of rain are likely across the \narea at times Saturday evening through Sunday night. Rainfall \namounts of 1-2 inches are expected across the area. Higher rain \ntotals are certainly possible, but will depend on a few other \nsmaller scale features. \n\nTropical moisture surges north into our area Saturday night into \nSunday, with the highest PWAT values at or above 2\" (most likely \neast of the Blue Ridge). While the main cutoff low associated with \nthe remnants of Isaias remain west of the Appalachians on Saturday, \nexpect light rain to move in from south to north in the evening. \nForcing for ascent increases Saturday night, and especially Sunday \nmorning into Sunday afternoon as mid-level heights fall with the \napproach of the cutoff low. There is going to be enhanced low-level \nconvergence somewhere over the region as the surface high to the \neast and cutoff low to the west interact. Easterly flow into the \nBlue Ridge could also produce enhanced rain amounts due to \norographic effects. Additionally, an inverted trough or wave of low \npressure tries to develop in lee of the Appalachians Sunday as it \ncrosses Virginia to the Delmarva.\n\nUncertainty remains regarding the exact evolution and timing of \nthese features, which will ultimately determine where / if any \nlocally heavy rain occurs. Continue to monitor the forecast over the \nnext couple of days. No Flood Watches are planned at this time as we \nare still over 48 hours from the main slug of rain. \n\nIt is going to be noticeably breezy, especially in the mountains \nwhere winds begin to pick up Saturday evening. The strongest winds \nare most likely on Sunday. The current forecast for peak wind gusts \nis 30-40 mph in the Alleghenies and 20-30 mph elsewhere.\n\nConditions dry out Monday morning into Monday afternoon as the \nremnants of Isaias push offshore. Looking at mostly mild \ntemperatures for most of next week. A series of cold fronts cross \nthe region during the middle to late part of next week as the \nsynoptic pattern becomes more active. Aside from mountain rain \nshowers, most of the area looks to remain dry. The strongest cold \nfront arrives late next week, possibly bringing rain and colder air \nbehind it. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /07Z FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nVFR conditions are expected through Saturday. Sub-VFR conditions are \nexpected at some time Saturday night into Sunday as the remnants of \nwhat is now Hurricane Isaias move through the area. Low clouds and \nreduced visibility due to steady rain are going to be the main \nimpacts. Breezy east winds gusting around 20 knots is likely on \nSunday. VFR conditions return Monday and Tuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nFavorable marine conditions are expected through Saturday afternoon. \nThen, SCA conditions are likely to develop across all the waters \nSaturday evening into Sunday as easterly flow increases. The \nremnants of what is now Hurricane Isaias will move across the waters \nSunday, which is when gusts of around 30 knots are likely. \nConditions improve Monday, though passing cold fronts next week will \nbring the opportunity for SCA conditions at times.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nMinor coastal flooding seems likely at sensitive locations Sunday \nevening due to rising water levels due to strengthening east to \nsoutheast winds combined with high astronomical tides due to a new \nmoon. Winds turn offshore Monday allowing tide levels to recede.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...KRR\nAVIATION...KRR\nMARINE...KRR\n"},"forecast":{"t":1791536489,"days":{"2026-10-09":{"hi":75,"lo":55,"pop":1,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-10":{"hi":73,"lo":61,"pop":83,"short":"Mostly Cloudy"},"2026-10-11":{"hi":69,"lo":64,"pop":99,"short":"Rain Showers"},"2026-10-12":{"hi":73,"lo":60,"pop":66,"short":"Showers And Thunderstorms Likely"},"2026-10-13":{"hi":78,"lo":59,"pop":14,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-14":{"hi":71,"lo":55,"pop":7,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-15":{"hi":68,"lo":null,"pop":7,"short":"Mostly Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1791536400,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,25000,null,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,24700,24400,24100,24300,24500,null,24800,24900,25000,25000,24900,24900,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,10000,10000,10000],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,1